WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.300
Republicans know that they didn't suppress
enough of the vote in twenty twenty.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:09.929
If they had suppressed enough of the vote
in November, Trump would be president right

00:00:09.929 --> 00:00:11.719
now instead of Joe Biden.

00:00:11.719 --> 00:00:16.219
So Republicans have launched a nationwide
voter suppression effort.

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:21.500
It's already underway with two hundred and
fifty three different bills in 43 states.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.380
Many of these bills premised on false.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:28.570
Claims that Donald Trump actually won the
2020 election, now you have to give them credit,

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:30.800
they see the problem and they're trying to
fix it.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:36.310
The problem is record voter turnout in November
gave the election to Joe Biden.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:39.430
As they see it, too many people voted.

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:42.610
So the solution is to prevent people from
voting.

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:46.490
Now, all of these bills in some way.

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:52.170
Attempt to restrict voting in ballot access,
the Brennan Center for Justice has a great

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:57.220
analysis on this, and the playbook includes
strategies we're familiar with make it more

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:01.230
difficult to register to vote by banning voter
registration.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.300
That's automatic by shortening registration
windows.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:06.179
That's one part of the playbook.

00:01:06.179 --> 00:01:08.700
Purge voters from voter rolls.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:12.009
So they believe they're registered, they show
up and they're not.

00:01:12.009 --> 00:01:16.450
You can do that by putting in place looser
and looser guidelines for when people can

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:20.649
be unregistered and removed from voter rolls.

00:01:20.649 --> 00:01:27.090
Third part, make voter ID requirements stricter
and stricter, which we know disproportionately

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:28.780
impacts poorer people.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.460
Now, how do they do this?

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:36.259
The way you do it, it's almost evil genius
in order to get the voter ID as free as they

00:01:36.259 --> 00:01:42.210
say, but in order to get the voter ID, you
require more and more documents that you have

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:46.070
to bring with you to be given the voter ID.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:51.369
And these are documents which poorer people
are less likely to have.

00:01:51.369 --> 00:01:55.920
People with more housing instability are less
likely to have retained.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:01.590
And some of the documents you need to get
your free voter ID costs money to get OK.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:05.609
And they're documents that take time to get
when people can't afford to take time off

00:02:05.609 --> 00:02:07.929
from work to deal with bureaucracy.

00:02:07.929 --> 00:02:13.840
So that's how you deal with this problem of
too many people voting through voter ID.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.790
Another approach is you add elements of voter
intimidation to voting.

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:20.060
You increase pollwatchers, that type of thing.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.090
A few examples.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:26.160
Georgia Senate Republicans have introduced
a bill that would end no excuse absentee voting

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.460
entirely, meaning you can't just say I want
to vote absentee because I want to.

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:34.190
They restrict the reasons that you can give
to vote absentee.

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:38.830
Georgia House Republicans have their own sweeping
bill, which would reduce the early voting

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:39.830
period.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.710
Again, just shortened the days, the number
of days people can vote.

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:46.690
Montana Republicans want to end same day voter
registration.

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:50.450
Missouri Republicans want a new voter ID requirement.

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:56.950
New Hampshire Republicans want to ban out-of-state
college students from voting in New Hampshire

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:59.390
and they want to end same day registration.

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:04.910
Iowa Republicans already approved a bill that
would shorten voting hours on and on and on.

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:10.410
Now, at the same time that there are bills
proposed to limit voting, there are also bills

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:13.480
proposed mostly by Democrats to expand voting
access.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:15.900
And hopefully some of those will pass.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.410
But Republicans aren't even pretending.

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:20.650
They haven't been pretending for a long time.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:25.500
They openly want to win, not by winning the
hearts and minds of people with policies that

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.050
voters will like.

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:31.820
They want to win by making it hard to vote
disproportionately for people that are statistically

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:35.360
more likely to vote for Democrats at some
point.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.620
And I believe that that point is now.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:41.990
The only way to deal with this is to pass
national election reform and to pass it in

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:47.640
a way that it overrides state limitations
that Republicans want to impose and to prevent

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.590
the next president from just undoing it.

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:55.020
Easier said than done, but is there any better
time to do it than when Democrats control

00:03:55.020 --> 00:04:00.410
the White House, the Senate and the House
of Representatives for at least the next two

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:01.410
years?

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:04.920
The time to do it has to be now, and we'll
be talking more about what that effort could

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.150
look like in the future.

